import{_ as t,o as s,c as o,ag as r}from"./chunks/framework.DC2M1AAK.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Field notes — what we learned building a production agent","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/lessons.md","filePath":"reference/lessons.md"}'),n={name:"reference/lessons.md"};function i(a,e,l,h,d,c){return s(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="field-notes-—-what-we-learned-building-a-production-agent" tabindex="-1">Field notes — what we learned building a production agent <a class="header-anchor" href="#field-notes-—-what-we-learned-building-a-production-agent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Field notes — what we learned building a production agent&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Every line below cost something to learn. They are grouped by the part of the system they shaped; the code cites them where it applies.</p><h2 id="models" tabindex="-1">Models <a class="header-anchor" href="#models" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Models&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A model cannot be told to be reliable.</strong> Instructions lower a slip rate; they never zero it. A model that filled a list &quot;for the user&quot; did so in 8 of 40 chats; the better model in 1 of 64 even with a note covering that case. The guard made it 0 for every model.</li><li><strong>Models differ in where they slip.</strong> One rejects a fixed temperature, one needs a thought signature echoed back, one sends buttons with no prose on skills tuned for another. Configure each endpoint separately; never run a lowest-common-denominator setting.</li><li><strong>Per-model notes belong to the endpoint</strong>, appended only when that model serves the call. One model&#39;s corrective notes are noise to another.</li><li><strong>Prompt text is not free.</strong> Three review paragraphs raised one scenario&#39;s flake rate from 0 in 16 to 4 in 8. Measure a prompt change over several runs and compare failing sets.</li><li><strong>Compare models on blended cost under YOUR prefix</strong>, not list price. With a ~40k-token cached prefix per call, the cache-read price and the cache-write surcharge decide the bill.</li><li><strong>A model run-to-run varies a lot</strong> (32–45 of 45 on identical code in one day). Never judge a change on one run; compare the sets of failing scenarios over three.</li><li><strong>Failover must happen before the first streamed token</strong>; afterwards a second endpoint would duplicate text. Keep the user&#39;s draft persisted so &quot;try again&quot; loses nothing.</li><li><strong>Reasoning-model knobs change what tools are allowed.</strong> Some endpoints accept function tools only with reasoning effort &quot;none&quot; on chat completions. Put the knob on the endpoint.</li></ul><h2 id="prompts-and-skills" tabindex="-1">Prompts and skills <a class="header-anchor" href="#prompts-and-skills" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Prompts and skills&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>One question per turn.</strong> Several questions in one message make the next reply unattributable; a bare value while several slots are open must be attributed explicitly.</li><li><strong>Never combine an options question with free-text asks</strong> in the same turn.</li><li><strong>Anything a record knows is shown and confirmed, never asked.</strong> The product moment is &quot;we already know your unit&quot;; letting it slide silently into the summary wastes it.</li><li><strong>Any choice from reference data with ≥2 options is chips or a list, never prose.</strong> An irrelevant chip is the real bug, not a lone chip.</li><li><strong>Say &quot;never announce the step after it&quot;; do not enumerate steps or give example phrases.</strong> Telling the model what the runtime does made it narrate &quot;photos, video and visibility&quot;.</li><li><strong>Describe the runtime&#39;s lines, never quote them.</strong> A model imitates lines it has seen in the history (&quot;Taking this as Sale. Right?&quot;) and re-asks them.</li><li><strong>Off-scope asks get &quot;that&#39;s outside what I can do here&quot;, never &quot;let&#39;s finish this first&quot;</strong> — the latter implies it could be done later. Declare capabilities from the installed domains.</li><li><strong>A tool parameter named like a UI heading invites a UI heading.</strong> <code>header</code> became &quot;Choose at least 5 key features&quot; and the reference lookup returned nothing.</li><li><strong>Load only the in-play variant&#39;s skill and tools.</strong> 103k → 44k characters per call, the suite 30% faster, rate-limit waits gone. Once the type is set, remove it from the tool&#39;s enum or the model re-sets it &quot;to be safe&quot; (27 of 37 calls).</li></ul><h2 id="reading-what-the-user-said" tabindex="-1">Reading what the user said <a class="header-anchor" href="#reading-what-the-user-said" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reading what the user said&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Read a reply by its WHOLE content, never its first word.</strong> 235 of 521 realistic messages were mis-read by a leading-word reader; 0 by the whole-content reader.</li><li><strong>Widening the yes-vocabulary opens the next hole.</strong> Add only words that cannot carry content; rewrite look-alike hand-offs into non-yes tokens; keep the create gate strictest.</li><li><strong>A lone number must be the whole message</strong> before it is a value (&quot;make it 3 bathrooms&quot; is not a price).</li><li><strong>Explicit words win over inference</strong> (a stated purpose beats a figure&#39;s size); questions are not statements; a message cut off after a connector is never a bare yes.</li><li><strong>A figure said with a money unit is not an id</strong> (&quot;40 L a month&quot; is not unit 40).</li><li><strong>A confirm of the shown value plus a plain remark settles;</strong> a question, a &quot;but&quot;, a second figure or a refusal does not.</li></ul><h2 id="runtime-owned-steps" tabindex="-1">Runtime-owned steps <a class="header-anchor" href="#runtime-owned-steps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Runtime-owned steps&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Validate tools are stateless:</strong> the runtime&#39;s settle call must send the whole typed draft or the problems list lies.</li><li><strong>A failed validate (an MCP error) must never read as a successful validate.</strong></li><li><strong>Carry settled values into every validate/preview/create call</strong>; a different value stands only when the user said it in words since.</li><li><strong>The draft the pane shows is built from the arguments the MODEL passed</strong>, so guards run on those arguments in place, not on a copy.</li><li><strong>Reset dependents on a change</strong>: price → the analytics verdict, furnishing → fixtures, type → specification, fewer photos → the video choice, project → everything below it.</li><li><strong>A runtime-answered turn is stored with its blocks</strong>, so a reload draws it again; the transcript endpoint draws the open ask on the last entry.</li><li><strong>A step waits for a complete draft</strong>; it never asks over an incomplete record.</li><li><strong>Every runtime moment emits a behaviour event with counts and enums only, never text.</strong></li><li><strong>Runtime-answered turns must not count against the per-session rate limit</strong>: a user tapping through five cards in a minute is the product working (a prod chat hit the limit on its 11th tap).</li></ul><h2 id="the-write-path" tabindex="-1">The write path <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-write-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The write path&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Probe first.</strong> The first backend accepted a create with EVERY field stripped (0 rejections in ~90 cases); misspelled duplicate keys were the real schema; a &quot;prod&quot; base URL shared the dev database; a <code>GET …/status/update/{id}</code> wrote; a POST to an unknown admin route created a record. Never probe unknown routes with POST; treat any 200 with an id as a write.</li><li><strong>The confirm gate ×3.</strong> The model attested <code>confirmed:true</code> after the user sent a change.</li><li><strong>Timeout + idempotency on the write</strong>, keyed by session + draft fingerprint; reconcile an ambiguous failure before creating again.</li><li><strong>No silent fuzzy fallback on the write path.</strong> Fuzzy matching is for suggestions; the write uses only ids the user confirmed.</li><li><strong>An environment write guard must not be a substring test</strong> (&quot;development&quot; in the host name). Use an explicit flag that must equal the literal &quot;true&quot;.</li><li><strong>Whole-document PUT:</strong> read, change only allow-listed paths, re-read right before the write, write, read back, say one line. Compare references by id (a GET may populate them).</li><li><strong>Create seeds its own status event</strong> when the server injects nothing — a record with an empty status history was invisible in every tab.</li></ul><h2 id="clients" tabindex="-1">Clients <a class="header-anchor" href="#clients" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Clients&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Upload ≠ attach.</strong> Files upload at once, keys wait on the session, attach the instant the write returns an id; the create payload ships an empty media block so a media hiccup never blocks the main write. Attach is idempotent. Bytes cross the MCP boundary as a temp file, never base64 over stdio.</li><li><strong>A model-emitted link block is XSS in every host page</strong> without a scheme allowlist.</li><li><strong>Fence junk is real:</strong> one block in forty came with junk glued to the closing fence, a two-backtick close, or no fence at all; the stream must hold back fence fragments.</li><li><strong>A model re-emits its pre-tool text after the tool answers</strong> — dedupe sentences and blocks.</li><li><strong>The user&#39;s bubble shows a readable echo of a form submit</strong>, not the wire text.</li><li><strong>Pin the widget&#39;s script URL to its version</strong>; a proxy&#39;s cache can hide a shipped fix for hours.</li><li><strong>&quot;List another&quot; = a new session</strong>; one chat = one job.</li><li><strong>A completed chat&#39;s replay must not resurrect link pills</strong> the live turn filtered out.</li><li><strong>Completion links are composed from today&#39;s templates</strong>, not the ones stored at create time.</li></ul><h2 id="sessions-and-stores" tabindex="-1">Sessions and stores <a class="header-anchor" href="#sessions-and-stores" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sessions and stores&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A session id must not be a bearer credential</strong>; with an open listing endpoint it was a full hijack chain. Owner checks answer 404.</li><li><strong>Any bearer string read any chat</strong> until identity was verified against the auth service. Verify, cache 60 s, fail closed.</li><li><strong>Never load every persisted session into RAM at startup</strong>; hydrate on miss, evict idle.</li><li><strong>A windowed history must never orphan a tool call from its result</strong>; both providers answer 400 forever.</li><li><strong>node-redis reconnects forever</strong>: bound the first connect or startup hangs on a down Redis.</li><li><strong>Any session-taking route that calls a tool must attach the request&#39;s bearer first</strong>; tokens are never persisted with sessions.</li><li><strong>A process whose stdout is a protocol channel must log to stderr</strong> (the MCP child&#39;s info lines were silently parsed as JSON-RPC noise).</li><li><strong>stdout is synchronous under Docker</strong>: log off the main thread.</li></ul><h2 id="observability-and-cost" tabindex="-1">Observability and cost <a class="header-anchor" href="#observability-and-cost" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Observability and cost&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A feature is done when it is visible on a panel</strong>: a span, a counter or behaviour event, a log line with the trace id, a panel when someone will look.</li><li><strong>OTLP/JSON failed on non-zero doubles with our backend; protobuf did not.</strong> Custom span attributes arrive as strings — cast in panel SQL. A field exists only after a span carried it, so always emit the attributes panels filter on, and prime counters at startup.</li><li><strong>Search the telemetry for a private string after building a private path</strong>; a note typed by the user turned up in a span input and a trace line until redaction covered every carrier.</li><li><strong>Users see a percentage of their cap, never tokens.</strong> Plan tokens (cost-weighted) track the bill with r = 1.00; raw totals varied 2.4×, billable-only 8.9×.</li><li><strong>Bill what vendors bill you</strong>; a cache hit on your own infrastructure costs nothing.</li></ul><h2 id="process" tabindex="-1">Process <a class="header-anchor" href="#process" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Process&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Evals before and after every change.</strong> When a check fails, decide out loud: check brittleness (fix the check) or a real regression (fix the agent) — and record which.</li><li><strong>Classify every red</strong>: green on a serial rerun = model flake (ledger it); red on a clean baseline worktree too = pre-existing; else a regression. A credit outage mid-run is a transport error, not a result.</li><li><strong>Shard the suite</strong>; 12 shards against a mock finish in minutes; 3 shards is the ceiling against a real provider&#39;s rate limit; 6 hit it.</li><li><strong>Run the full gate, not a targeted subset</strong>: it caught two regressions the unit tests missed.</li><li><strong>Mutation checks prove the checks bite</strong>: text mutants in scratch copies of the repo.</li><li><strong>The eval runner&#39;s own copy of the rules goes stale</strong>: share the reader with the runtime.</li><li><strong>Do not run the gateway while editing readers</strong>; it loads code at start.</li><li><strong>One branch per fix; never push to a branch with an open PR.</strong> Three races with merges.</li><li><strong>Never give hour-scale estimates unprompted</strong>; give the parallel number and what is cut.</li><li><strong>Fix the class in code, not the instance</strong>; add an every-scenario check for it.</li><li><strong>Don&#39;t ask for confirmation on small, obvious things</strong> inside the branch; ask for real decisions, pushes, flags that change what ships.</li><li><strong>Unattended device work touches nothing private</strong>; credentials stay the owner&#39;s to type.</li></ul><hr><p><small>Source: <a href="https://github.com/marg-adk/marg/blob/main/docs/LESSONS.md">docs/LESSONS.md</a> — edit it there; the site mirrors the repository.</small></p>',22)])])}const p=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
